<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalda Night</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yaldā Night, Chilla Night, Chelle Night, Shab-e Yalda, Shab-e Chilla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yalda Night, also called Chilla Night, is an ancient festival celebrated on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> of the Northern Hemisphere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. On the longest night of the year friends and family gather to eat, drink and read poetry, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, late into the night; pomegranates and watermelons are particularly significant. In Zoroastrian tradition the night was inauspicious, and its customs were originally intended to protect people from evil. The name Yalda was borrowed from Syriac-speaking Christians, in whose language it means &quot;birth&quot;. It was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Intangible Cultural Heritage Lists in December 2022.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yalda-night.jpg" alt="Yalda Night" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vfnyos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L04vb2pizbcs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Yalda Night was one of the holy nights of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and was included in the official calendar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from at least 502 BCE, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. The researcher Zana Salehrad traces the celebration to solar rituals of pre-Zoroastrian times marking the rebirth of the sun on the longest night of the year. In Zoroastrian tradition the night was inauspicious, because the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahriman">Ahriman</a> were imagined to be at their peak. People stayed awake for most of it, lest misfortune befall them, and gathered in groups of friends and relatives to share the last fruits of summer. The next day, the first of the month of Dae, was one of celebration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name Chilla, meaning fortieth, comes from the night's opening of the forty-day period of the great Chilla at the start of winter. Yalda is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> word meaning birth. Eastern Christians had settled in Parthian and Sasanian territory, and through them Iranians came into contact with the Syriac Christian name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>; Shab-e Yalda and Shab-e Chilla gradually became synonymous.</p><p>Today extended families gather for a dinner with fruits and sweetmeats prepared for the night, including watermelon, pomegranate, nuts and dried fruit, often set on a korsi; in some areas forty kinds of edibles are served. Eating watermelon on this night is said to protect against the heat and disease of summer. After dinner older people tell tales, and a favourite pastime is fal-e <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, divination by opening the Divan of Hafez, which should not be done more than three times lest the poet become angry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Poems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> are read aloud, and people stay up past midnight talking and telling jokes. The tradition of lighting the house and yard with candles has largely lapsed since the spread of electricity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> the sweet kafbikh is prepared, and a gift of dried fruits and nuts is given to the bride.</p><p>In 1979 the journalist Hashem Razi suggested that Mehregan, the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>, had shifted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> in early Islamic times; his documentation has been accepted but his conclusion has not. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>'s Chella, a forty-day retreat of fasting, is unrelated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yalda Night” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yalda Night</b>, also called Chilla Night, is celebrated in Iran, Kurdistan, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Afghanistan, Azerbaijan and Turkmenistan.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
